  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 AM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nThe weekend opens on a much more tranquil note than 24-hours ago \nwhen the much welcomed round of moderate to heavy rain was taking \nresidence over the forecast area. Fast forward to early this morning \nand water vapor imagery displays the departing upper-low moving \nthrough central Iowa en-route toward the Ohio Valley. The last of \nthe raindrops have ended in western WI and the back edge of the \ncloud shield is gradually moving east through central MN. Nighttime \nGOES RGB channel does display some holes in the cloud deck over far \neastern MN into western WI, which has accelerated boundary layer \ncooling and supported the development of patchy fog (at times \ndense). Light winds and Friday's rainfall will keep instances \nof patchy fog possible through daybreak, however the lingering \ncloud cover does introduce some uncertainty in how widespread \nfog will be. \n\nFog will erode shortly after daybreak and will be followed up by a \nbeautiful weekend and start to August! The Upper Midwest sits in a \nregion of large scale subsidence behind the departing low and the \nexpansion of a ridge from the west. Comfortable high temperatures in \nthe low to mid 80s will be the theme across the forecast area today \nand tomorrow under mostly sunny skies. As was previously discussed, \nit may be a little hazy on the horizon owing to smoke in the upper-\nlevels of the atmosphere, but even that looks to be limited through \nthe weekend. The final piece of the pleasant weekend forecast is the \narrival of drier air, thus less humidity thanks to northerly surface \nflow.\n\nEarly next week, a progressive trough supported by an active 60-70kt \nPacific jet will advance across central Canada. The core of an upper-\nlow embedded within the trough will remain well to the north of\nthe International Border, however an associated trailing cold \nfront will extend south across the northern CONUS. The front \nwill be the focus for thunderstorm development Monday evening \nacross the eastern Dakotas & northwestern MN. SPC has highlighted\nthe Red River Valley in a Slight Risk (Level 2/5) where frontal\nforcing will combine with sufficient moisture pooling ahead of \nthe boundary to yield CAPEs in the 1500-2000 J/kg range supportive\nof severe convection. Latest guidance suite displays a more \nmodest moisture picture to the southeast of the Slight Risk into\nour forecast area, which should support weakening convection as\nit advances eastward late Monday night into Tuesday morning. \nSPC did include a Marginal Risk (Level 1/5) across west-central \nMN with the new Day 3 Outlook which represents the potential for\nan isolated damaging wind gust or instance of large hail. NBM's\nPoPs capture this idea, displaying 60-80% PoPs from Yellow \nMedicine to Todd counties late Monday night. PoPs fall to just\n20-30% over eastern MN & western WI by daybreak Tuesday. The \nfront is progged to sag southward across the forecast area \nTuesday and will force renewed convective chances along the \nMN/IA border during the second half of the day. Putting the \npieces together, it looks like western MN has the best chance \nfor thunderstorms later Monday evening, but if current trends \ncontinue much of the forecast will miss out on meaningful \nprecipitation from this frontal passage. High pressure returns \nWednesday and temperatures aim to run near or just below normal \nin the upper 70s to lower 80s for the middle of the week.",
